Build option MESEN_ZERO_WS_ROWS: the WonderSwan's picture chip starts with
its row buffers zeroed.

WsPpu::_rowData (the two rows of pixels being drawn) had no initial value,
and the first frame read a row before any line had been drawn into it: the
first picture of a WonderSwan game depended on what the memory held before
(valgrind: "conditional jump depends on uninitialised value", allocated in
WsConsole::LoadRom). The picture of Saint Seiya's first frame differed
between a native build and the sandbox in 70 pixels of line 127. With this
option the rows start at zero. Off, nothing changes.

--- a/Core/WS/WsPpu.h
+++ b/Core/WS/WsPpu.h
@@ -31,7 +31,13 @@
 		uint8_t Priority;
 	};
 
+#ifdef MESEN_ZERO_WS_ROWS
+	//Zeroed: the first frame read a row before any line had been drawn into
+	//it, so the picture depended on what the memory held before
+	PixelData _rowData[2][224] = {};
+#else
 	PixelData _rowData[2][224];
+#endif
 
 	uint16_t _screenHeight = 0;
 	uint16_t _screenWidth = 0;
